 3.2 · Where the lock's testimony did NOT reproduce — recorded, not hidden
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  The lock is testimony. Three of its §5.2 findings did not survive being
  reproduced against the rendered page, and are recorded here as false
  positives so the implementer does not "fix" something that is not broken.

  (a) Row 5, "About dimension item — hover: —".  FALSE POSITIVE.
      The item has no `:hover` rule, which is what a source read shows, but it
      carries `onMouseEnter={() => setActive(index)}` (About.tsx:93) and
      `.item[data-active]` (About.module.css:150) then repaints the top rule and
      the index numeral. TC-STATE-HOVER measures the rendered result and finds
      13 changed computed properties on hover. The state is designed; it is
      simply designed in JavaScript. Confirmed missing hover is rows 7, 11, 18
      and 20 only — four, not five.

  (b) Row 23, "MiniVic controls — active: —" and the totals line
      "`:active` designed on 0 of 23".  PARTIALLY FALSE.
      The launcher carries `active:scale-95` (MiniVicBot.tsx:1530) and is the
      one element on the site that already presses. TC-STATE-ACTIVE finds 20 of
      21, not 21 of 21. The lock's own row 23 focus finding, by contrast,
      reproduced exactly: every control inside the panel falls through to
      Chromium's `outline-style: auto`, and the composer input has its ring
      removed outright.

  (c) The empty-state subjects named in §5.3 are not all reachable.
      "Skills filter returning no rows" cannot happen with the shipped data:
      app/data/portfolio/skills.ts holds 14 production, 2 non-production and 1
      pending capability, and the three filters are all / production / pending,
      so the smallest result any filter can return is 1. "Avatar transcript
      before load" cannot be empty either — avatarContent.transcript is a static
      array compiled into the bundle. Neither can be exercised through the UI,
      so per §6 of the operating contract they are verified at the boundary
      instead: IS-04 asserts the zero-length branch exists in source for the two
      collections whose length really is decided at runtime (the filtered
      capability table and the clone's transcript log), and the end-to-end path
      is recorded here as UNVERIFIED. The static content arrays are deliberately
      out of scope: an empty state that can never be reached is dead code, and
      TC-NFR-DEADCSS exists to keep dead code off this site.

  (d) Row 22, the service-worker toast's Reload button, has no runtime subject.
      It renders only when the worker reports a waiting update, which a static
      export served fresh never does. Its missing `:focus-visible` is therefore
      audited statically (IS-01/IS-02 scan app/globals.css and every component)
      and its runtime behaviour is recorded as UNVERIFIED.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 3.3 · Two findings the lock did not record
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  (e) app/globals.css:451 sets `outline: none` inside
      `[data-outcome-card="true"]:hover, [data-outcome-card="true"]:focus-visible`
      — a focus ring removed with no replacement. The selector matches nothing
      the source renders today (no `data-outcome-card` attribute exists in app/
      or components/), so it is both an R-90 anti-pattern and dead CSS. The same
      is true of `#terminal-input` (globals.css:770) and `#mini-vic-input`
      (globals.css:789, 800) — neither id is rendered anywhere.

  (f) `* { cursor: auto }` at app/globals.css:125 is unlayered, so it beats
      Tailwind's preflight and every element that is not a `<button>` renders
      the browser's default cursor. TC-STATE-CURSOR measures 45 of 97 — every
      anchor and every `tabIndex=0` list item on the page. The lock describes
      the cursor-state machine as "declared but not implemented"; this is the
      measured size of that gap.

